{"id": "fy1-consecutive-product-even", "field": "number-theory", "tier": "easy", "language": "en", "trivial": false, "request": "Show that n(n+1) is even for every integer n.", "expected_conclusion": "任意の整数 n について n(n+1) は偶数。", "fast_outcome": "fallthrough", "fast_reason": "explanation_rejected", "kind": "prove", "answer": null, "tactic": "model_0", "formal": "∀ n : ℤ, Even (n * (n + 1))", "proved": "∀ n : ℤ, Even (n * (n + 1))", "explanation": "Let $n$ be an arbitrary integer. The proof uses the fact that every integer is either even or odd, and handles the two possibilities separately.\n\nIf $n$ is even, write $n=2k$ for some integer $k$. Then $n(n+1)=2k(2k+1)=2\bigl(k(2k+1)\bigr)$, so the product is twice an integer and is therefore even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, write $n=2k+1$ for some integer $k$. Then $n+1=2(k+1)$, so $n(n+1)=(2k+1)2(k+1)=2\bigl((2k+1)(k+1)\bigr)$, which is again twice an integer and hence even. These cases exhaust all integers $n$.\n\nTherefore, $n(n+1)$ is even for every integer $n$.", "explanation_approved": false, "explanation_issue": "rejected:The learner-visible proof contains malformed mathematical notation: the two occurrences intended as \\bigl and \\bigr appear as control characters in the displayed proof, so the expressions are not vali", "gate": {"passed": true, "reasons": []}, "fast_seconds": {"first_call": 4.07, "closed": 4.606, "verified": 5.319, "gate": 7.516, "explained": 8.138, "explanation_review": 3.203, "reviewed": 11.341, "total": 11.341}, "winner": "fallback", "total_s": 80.537, "fast_usd": 0.0, "normal": {"ok": true, "repairs": 1, "wall_s": 68.164, "answer_s": 80.53615565109067, "usd": 0.0315}, "calls": [{"label": "merged", "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "none", "prompt_chars": 1351, "input_tokens": 431, "output_tokens": 162, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.07, "seconds": 4.07, "error": null}, {"label": "merged", "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "none", "prompt_chars": 1351, "input_tokens": 431, "output_tokens": 264, "start": 0.001, 